    "role": "assistant",
    "content": "*Just a few more leagues, and the world will finally bow to a mind like mine.*\n\nSand ground against his heels with every step. He adjusted his cloak, the silk smooth against the grit of the Qaresi border. Here, the dunes surrendered to the savannah, where shrubs clawed at the sky. \n\nHe shielded his map from the wind. Moonlight washed over the parchment, casting a shadow from his own ears across diagrams and eroded landmarks.\n\n“Where are you?” he whispered. The words barely rose above the wind. “A man of your stature shouldn't be so difficult to find.”\n\nSomewhere out there lay the Vizier—a man who had reached for a crown and fallen into the dust. To the Qaresi, the man was a traitor. To the elf, he was something much more.\n\n*They bury their dead with such sickening devotion. All these tombs, all these rituals... they do it out of 'respect.' Such pathetic beliefs. They build monuments to ghosts and call it honor. They don't realize they are building libraries of servants.*\n\nHe squinted toward the horizon; he was where he should be. The environment was playing tricks on him, surely. If he were in the deep desert, the Ley Lines would have bled into the earth, coaxing strange vegetation from the sand to mark such qi rich Qaresi tombs. Instead, the scrubland acted as a shroud, hiding the magical signatures he needed to track.\n\n*Where is it?! WHERE IS IT?!*\n\nThe magister had been a prodigy at the Methenas Academy. He had fled kingdoms to claim a destiny. He should not be wandering through such wasteland like a merchant. He considered calling upon his black ice magic to freeze the ground, but he remained still. Discipline was the mark of a master.\n\nOnce more, he turned to the map. The few stories he had found had nothing but melodramatic nonsense and vague landmarks. *What if...*\n\nJust then a movement caught his eye.\n\nBetween the silhouettes of the shrubs and the low hills, a light pulsed far away. It lacked the steady chill of the moon or the glow of a star. It was dim.\n\n*A spirit?* He looked toward the glow. *Is it a camp? Or another seeker? No, there is nothing on the charts for this area.*\n\nThe light shifted. It drifted, swaying as if someone carried it through the dark.\n\n*It’s... moving.*"
