    "role": "assistant",
    "content": "The magister crept toward the canyon’s edge and shadow-stepped behind twisted branches and jagged rocks. He kept his breathing shallow until he reached the shelter of a gnarled acacia. He peered out, expecting perhaps a ritual lamp or a stray elemental.\n\nInstead, he found something infinitely more ridiculous.\n\nIt was a bird—an ostrich, by the looks of it—but it was utterly absurd. “What am I even looking at?” Its neck was covered in jewelry, it had long straight hair and immaculate bangs framing its forehead, and the amber light came from glowing hieroglyphs on its body. “All this travel, and all I come across is a glorified chicken.” Never had his eyes seem anything like this before. The sight was odd enough to summon a chuckle out of him.\n\nHe reached into his robes and retrieved a leather-bound volume on Qaresi natural history. After flipping through pages of cramped script, he found an entry. _Nyw,_ the text read. _Daughters born of the sacred lightning of Atahuti, the Lady of the Sky. Beautiful like the women of the Qaresi Empire._ “So that explains why their women look like this. The people of this place are so idiotic they inspire themselves in mindless animals.” He turned the page.\n\n_Like their mother, they are fond of shiny trinkets, much like the stars that dot the sky. O Atahuti, beautiful are the nyw that in your honor harne—_\n\n“There is nothing of note here.” He slammed the book shut. “This entire empire is built on primitive superstition.” Turning on his heel, he headed southeast to continue his search toward the ley line convergences. This entire expedition was proving to be a circus in time wasting.\n\n**CRACK.**\n\nA sound reverberated through the ravine like a crossbow bolt hitting a shield; loud enough to stop him on his tracks.\n\nThe creature was still in the clearing, but the ground beneath its powerful feet had fractured. The nyw seemed delighted by its own destruction. It stamped its legs with increasing ferocity against a particular patch of packed sand and limestone.\n\n**CRUNCH.**\n\nAnother impact followed, closer than the last, accompanied by the rumble of shifting earth. A section of ancient stone, likely hidden for centuries beneath a thin veneer of sediment, gave way entirely.\n\nAfter an even louder collapse, a gaping hole yawned open in the darkness.\n\n“No... no way!” *Could it be?* “Impossible!”\n\nThe nyw let out several curious chirps as it stared its own work. Soon enough, it effortlessly hopped into the chasm it created.\n\n“Get back here you stupid thing!” He sprinted toward the opening. “How does a mindless bird find it before a prodigy of Szona?”\n\nThe dust cloud settling around the pit was acrid with the smell of disturbed earth and something older—something cold and stale. It was too clean, too deliberate to be a natural sinkhole.\n\nHe reached the precipice and peered over the edge. Through the swirling curtain of disturbed dust, something caught the moonlight: faded, intricately carved hieroglyphs etched into stone walls, half-buried and caked in centuries of grime. The walls slanted inward, forming a passage that descended into the bowels of the earth.\n\n*Yes! This is it, it has to be! Sutentet’s tomb!*\n\nHis prize lay beneath his boots, exposed by nothing more than a bird’s tantrum. The nyw was nowhere to be seen; it had vanished deeper into the abyss, leaving only the inviting silence of the grave."
