    "role": "assistant",
    "content": "The magister leaped. He descended into the mouth of the chasm, his boots skidding against the slope of loose sand. He landed hard, a spray of grit coating his ankles, but he stayed upright as his cloak settled around him. Below, the darkness swallowed the moonlight.\n\n“**Lux,**” he commanded. A whirlpool of light coalesced near his shoulder. It bobbed with his movement, casting steady shadows against the stone.\n\nAs the light expanded, the scale of the tomb emerged. Pillars lined the hall, their surfaces carved with murals in the Qaresi style. Figures appeared in profile, accompanied by lines of hieroglyphs he could not decipher.\n\nBut the images told a story he could understand. They depicted Sutentet in his prime: overseeing the weighing of grain, sitting by a riverbank, consulting with scribes. It was a chronicle of a man’s life, a testament to a former pillar of the empire. *The dedication is excessive. To build such grandeur for a man who sought to upend the throne? Is this how they treat all their dead, even the traitors?*\n\nThe murals shifted. The scenes of daily life gave way to a woman. She was painted with fine detail, her hair a shade of midnight, her eyes holding a piercing emerald light. She was beautiful, and the art made sure to represent her so. She was by Sutentet's side at every turn.\n\n*His wife.* The magister chuckled at her image and looked away. *A mere distraction. A woman of flesh and emotion. There is no power to be harvested from a ghost of romance.*\n\nHis attention turned to a larger mural at a junction in the hall. This scene was different—it depicted Sutentet and his wife, but a third figure kneeled before them. Her hair framed her forehead in midnight black, like the ostrich, but short. Her silhouette was defined by armor and twin daggers at her waist.\n\nA warrior. \n\nHe pulled a leather lexicon from his robes; this could be worth translating. He held his light close and traced the hieroglyphs that framed the scene.\n\n**“To Bazeteb, Medjay to the Vizier and his Lady. When the shadows lengthened and the end drew nigh, her blades sang their final song in defense of her masters. She guarded them until the sands claimed her glory, and for this her valor shall be etched into the very stone of the earth.”**\n\nAgain? The sorcerer stared at the image of the crouched warrior.  *Why? If he was a traitor to the Pharaoh, why does this tomb weep for him? Why honor the woman who died defending a rebel?*\n\nThe mystery of the Qaresi’s reverence remained, but the magister had no time for theology. *The Vizier is waiting.*"
